# Production Principles

When generating or refactoring Python, follow these. Prefer the simplest design that
meets the task — do not over-engineer for speculative needs (YAGNI). Align with Felix
scope in `specification/product.mdc`.

## 1. Single responsibility & cohesion

One reason to change per function/class. Do not mix validate + persist + notify in one unit.

## 2. Encapsulation & abstraction

Hide internals (`_private`). Expose a small public surface. Prefer methods/actions over
reaching into another object's private state. Avoid Java-style getter/setter boilerplate
when a clear attribute or method is enough.

## 3. Loose coupling & modularity

Depend on interfaces (`typing.Protocol`), not concrete classes hardcoded inside callers.
Inject collaborators so implementations can be swapped in tests.

## 4. Open for extension

Prefer adding a new small type/module over growing a monolithic `elif` chain — when
extension is a real requirement, not speculative.

## 5. Portability

No hardcoded secrets or machine-specific paths. Env for credentials; `pathlib.Path` for files.

## 6. Defensive programming

Validate inputs at boundaries. Use type hints. Prefer immutable results
(`frozen` dataclasses / Pydantic models) for data crossing layers.

## 7. Testability

Break complex work into small pieces. Keep side effects at the edges so pure logic
is easy to unit test.

## 8. Simplicity (KISS, DRY, YAGNI)

Reuse recurring logic. Do not add features, layers, or abstractions that were not asked for.
Write the most straightforward code that satisfies the acceptance criteria.
